    "result_text": "All green. Here's how I addressed each finding.\n\n**Finding 1 \u2014 `CapMembershipLevel` mutating siblings' `householdSize`: fixed (with one correction).**\nThe correction: `householdSize` is `@Transient`, so those writes never reach the database \u2014 the \"persists under transaction\" rationale doesn't hold. But the underlying point is right: a `Cap\u2026` step shouldn't be reaching into other entities' state at all. I removed the mutation. `Owner` now exposes a read-only `membershipLevelForHouseholdSize(Integer)` (the existing `getMembershipLevel()`/`getMembershipPoints()` were refactored to route through it), so `CapMembershipLevel` derives each member's level for the current household size without touching any member's field. `householdSize` stays solely owned by `CountHouseholdMembers`.\n\n**Finding 2 \u2014 third copy of the `findAll()`+`Household.id` loop: fixed.**\nAdded `Household.membersOf(lastName, postcode, repository)` as the single member-collection definition, next to the rest of the household identity logic. `CountHouseholdMembers` and `CapMembershipLevel` now both call it. I also pointed `CheckHouseholdUnique` at it (keeping its soft-delete skip), even though that step is currently unwired, so the pattern lives in exactly one place. (Minor note: that loop wasn't quite \"verbatim\" \u2014 it skipped deleted owners and short-circuited \u2014 so the helper includes deleted members and each caller filters as needed.)\n\n**Finding 3 \u2014 persisted cap vs. otherwise-derived level: documented.**\nExpanded the `membershipLevelCap` field javadoc to state the split is deliberate: points and household size are recomputed per read, but the cap is a creation-time snapshot because the rule concerns the household *as it stood when the owner joined* \u2014 so it's persisted and reapplied rather than re-derived from current membership.\n\nAcceptance behaviour is unchanged \u2014 `AcceptanceTest.coreCapsLevelByHousehold` still passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, the two household owners come out at level 1 and a capped level 2).",
